  "summary": "# Daily Health-Tech Digest\n\nArtificial intelligence continues reshaping clinical operations, with federal regulators now convening closed-door meetings with tech companies and lobbyists to accelerate clinical AI adoption. Meanwhile, AI-powered clinical trial platforms are gaining traction—QuantHealth raised $45 million for its trial simulation technology aimed at reducing the industry's 90 percent failure rate, and Grove AI's voice agent recruitment tool moved from startup to acquisition in just two years. Digital health applications are expanding in wound care through smartphone video monitoring and AI-supported mental health episode prediction via Google Health Enterprise, though patient surveys indicate strong preference for AI supporting rather than replacing clinician judgment.\n\nThe FDA approved Takeda's novel narcolepsy drug, opening doors for an entirely new class of treatments, while Apnimed's $192 million IPO funds development of what could become the first oral sleep apnea medication. Separately, Attovia Therapeutics raised $289 million through an upsized IPO to advance immune disorder biologics. On the policy front, regulators are reviving a controversial 340B rebate program despite provider pushback, and analysis suggests exempting rare disease drugs from Medicare drug-pricing pilots would eliminate significant savings. Erica Schwartz was confirmed as CDC director, ending a nearly yearlong vacancy, while a Senate inquiry is examining whether the Trump administration softened kratom and tobacco policies in a quid pro quo arrangement.",
